  "medSchoolStudLife": {
    "medicalSchoolId": 27130,
    "diversity": "The Office of Multicultural and Community Affairs (OMCA) focuses on promoting and facilitating an inclusive environment for instruction, research, and health care delivery at the UConn School of Medicine (SOM) in alignment with the school\u2019s commitment to diversity and inclusion.  OMCA aims to be a resource for the SOM and UConn Health communities, particularly for those representing diversity in all its varied expressions, including but not limited to, gender, race, ethnicity, physical ability, sexual orientation, and gender identity.  Our programming and initiatives, grounded in amplifying diversity and inclusion, cultivate leadership, integrity, and engaged citizenship among our students, faculty and staff.",
    "leaveOfAbsence": null,
    "organizationActivities": "Numerous opportunities available, with over 100 active student organizations and interest groups.",
    "setting": "The 162 acre Health Center is seven miles west of Hartford, with panoramic views of rural hills and the Hartford skyline. The John Dempsey University Hospital is onsite. Extensive research facilities are an integral part of the Health Center.",
    "housing": "The University's Off-Campus Housing Office helps students identify housing in the local community.",
    "supportSystems": "\"Reach Out\" is the student organization for LGBTQ students.",
    "supportSystemsForDisabilities": "https://medicine.uconn.edu/student-life/information/",
    "wellnessPrograms": "UConn's Wellness Committee provides programming with the triple aim of wellness promotion, burnout prevention, and resiliency training.  Although individual programs and events are important components of a student wellness plan, the ultimate goal of the Wellness Committee is to foster a culture of wellness among students, faculty, and staff so that wellness becomes a central, rather than supplemental, part of the curriculum.  During the first two years of  the MDelta Curriculum, which includes both medical and dental students, this will take the form of a longitudinal curriculum that provides students with concrete self-care skills including training in mindfulness-based stress reduction, periodic mental health checks, opportunities for regular physical activity, and creation of a network of supportive relationships with other students, faculty and staff. During third and fourth year, the emphasis will be on carving out protected time for wellness in the midst of the stressors of clerkship rotations and residency applications. The wellness skills that the students gain through this curriculum will ideally readily translate to active wellness promotion and burnout prevention as they embark upon residency training.",
    "campusCulture": "https://medicine.uconn.edu/about-us/",
